WEBVTT

00:00:07.393 --> 00:00:12.694
Today we're looking at our new widget plant
being built at Southside. I've asked Barbara

00:00:12.694 --> 00:00:18.863
to report on progress and bring us up to date
and up to speed. Barbara?

00:00:18.863 --> 00:00:25.184
Thanks Denise. I'll just outline the process
we've been through, identify some problems,

00:00:25.184 --> 00:00:29.604
and give you an estimate on completion time
and the outcome financially.

00:00:29.604 --> 00:00:31.644
Is it good news or bad news?

00:00:31.644 --> 00:00:37.573
Bear with me. Now, if you recall, after a
feasibility study, we put the project out

00:00:37.573 --> 00:00:43.403
to tender eighteen months ago, and selected
Ezybuild as our project manager.

00:00:43.403 --> 00:00:49.394
Work commenced about fifteen months ago, and
it's been progressing to schedule until recently.

00:00:49.394 --> 00:00:51.774
What's the problem?

00:00:51.774 --> 00:00:58.634
Unfortunately there are three: Firstly, there's
been a delay in materials - specifically steel

00:00:58.634 --> 00:01:05.113
because of industrial issues at the suppliers.
Secondly, we've lost days due to the weather.

00:01:05.113 --> 00:01:09.903
And finally, there's been a resulting cost
blowout.

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:10.964
So what are we going to do?

00:01:10.964 --> 00:01:15.784
Well, they've managed to get another supplier
now. I suggested moving the completion date

00:01:15.784 --> 00:01:21.324
back. That way, there's no penalty, and they
agreed to re-deploy their workers until building

00:01:21.324 --> 00:01:22.324
can start again.

00:01:22.324 --> 00:01:25.314
Smart thinking.

00:01:25.314 --> 00:01:29.804
We've been waiting for the rain to stop - but
we can't control the weather!

00:01:29.804 --> 00:01:31.474
And the cost?

00:01:31.474 --> 00:01:35.934
At this stage, just a small overage. But I'll
be watching it very closely over the next

00:01:35.934 --> 00:01:41.824
few months. With no more delays, we're expecting
to complete the project just one month behind

00:01:41.824 --> 00:01:42.824
schedule.

00:01:42.824 --> 00:01:46.574
Good work Barbara.

00:01:46.574 --> 00:01:56.114
Humph
Today's episode is a focussed meeting with

00:01:56.114 --> 00:02:03.273
a specific purpose. Barbara has been asked
to report on the progress of a project. Our

00:02:03.273 --> 00:02:10.984
focus today is on the verb tenses she uses
to report. Firstly, let's look at how Denise

00:02:10.984 --> 00:02:15.734
asks for Barbara's report.
Today we're looking today at our new widget

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:21.074
plant being built at Southside. I've asked
Barbara to report on progress and bring us

00:02:21.074 --> 00:02:26.235
all up to date and up to speed.
Denise says, "Today we're looking at our new

00:02:26.235 --> 00:02:28.294
widget plant."

00:02:28.294 --> 00:02:34.273
She uses the present continuous tense, 'we're
looking' or 'we are looking' because she's

00:02:34.273 --> 00:02:39.844
telling them what they are doing, and what
they are going to do at the meeting now.

00:02:39.844 --> 00:02:45.004
She doesn't use the simple present 'we look',
because that is used for regular actions.

00:02:45.004 --> 00:02:50.144
Then she says, "I've asked Barbara to report."

00:02:50.144 --> 00:02:55.684
She uses the present perfect tense, 'I have
asked' because she asked Barbara to report

00:02:55.684 --> 00:03:00.554
before the meeting, and Barbara is about to
give her report.

00:03:00.554 --> 00:03:04.314
We'll look more at present perfect later.

00:03:04.314 --> 00:03:08.954
And she wants Barbara to bring them 'up to
date' and 'up to speed'.

00:03:08.954 --> 00:03:15.194
These are common expressions. To bring someone
'up to date' is to tell them what has happened

00:03:15.194 --> 00:03:21.454
up to the present. And to bring someone 'up
to speed' is to make sure they know all the

00:03:21.454 --> 00:03:23.714
relevant facts.

00:03:23.714 --> 00:03:28.694
How does Barbara respond?
Thanks Denise. I'll just outline the process

00:03:28.694 --> 00:03:34.434
we've been through, identify some problems,
and give you an estimate on completion time

00:03:34.434 --> 00:03:40.764
and the outcome financially.
She says, "I'll just outline the process."

00:03:40.764 --> 00:03:45.993
She uses the future tense, I will, because
she's talking about something she's going

00:03:45.993 --> 00:03:53.023
to do in the next few minutes. Notice that
the 'will' is not repeated, but it applies

00:03:53.023 --> 00:03:58.193
to all three of the things she says she is
going to do.

00:03:58.193 --> 00:04:04.583
Let's see how Barbara reports on progress.
Now, if you recall, after a feasibility study,

00:04:04.583 --> 00:04:09.633
we put the project out to tender eighteen
months ago, and selected Ezybuild as our project

00:04:09.633 --> 00:04:13.574
manager.
Because Barbara is describing events in the

00:04:13.574 --> 00:04:20.074
past, she uses the simple past tense.
We put the project out to tender.

00:04:20.074 --> 00:04:24.394
We selected Ezybuild as the project manager.

00:04:24.394 --> 00:04:28.813
These events happened in the past, and they
are finished.

00:04:28.813 --> 00:04:34.864
Work commenced about fifteen months ago, and
it's been progressing to schedule until recently.

00:04:34.864 --> 00:04:41.354
Again we see the simple past in the phrase:
Work commenced about fifteen months ago. The

00:04:41.354 --> 00:04:48.914
work started at a particular time in the past.
But look at the next phrase: "It's been progressing

00:04:48.914 --> 00:04:53.634
to schedule."
When we look at continuous events - things

00:04:53.634 --> 00:05:00.304
that happen over a period of time, we use
a continuous tense. The work started in the

00:05:00.304 --> 00:05:06.534
past, and it has continued until the present.
This is called the present perfect continuous

00:05:06.534 --> 00:05:16.474
tense. It's here is short for, it has. Try
some other examples with Barbara.

00:05:16.474 --> 00:05:24.044
Work's been going on since last year.

00:05:24.044 --> 00:05:30.484
We've been monitoring progress continuously.

00:05:30.484 --> 00:05:38.274
I've been checking the work regularly.
Now let's look at how Barbara describes the

00:05:38.274 --> 00:05:43.613
three problems.
Firstly, there's been a delay in materials

00:05:43.613 --> 00:05:49.074
- specifically steel because of industrial
issues at the suppliers. Secondly, we've lost

00:05:49.074 --> 00:05:55.183
days due to the weather. And finally, there's
been a cost blowout.

00:05:55.183 --> 00:05:58.563
Notice the verb tense Barbara uses.

00:05:58.563 --> 00:06:03.373
there's been a delay
we've lost days

00:06:03.373 --> 00:06:06.873
there's been a cost blowout

00:06:06.873 --> 00:06:12.713
These are all present perfect verbs, using
'has' or 'have'.

00:06:12.713 --> 00:06:17.264
there has been
we have lost

00:06:17.264 --> 00:06:22.034
Present perfect tense is used to describe
events which began in the past and are still

00:06:22.034 --> 00:06:24.384
true now.

00:06:24.384 --> 00:06:30.713
In business it can be important to use the
correct verb tense. Using the wrong one can

00:06:30.713 --> 00:06:38.074
change the meaning. For example, if Barbara
said, "There was a delay", it means this delay

00:06:38.074 --> 00:06:43.134
happened in the past, and there is no delay
now.

00:06:43.134 --> 00:06:48.644
If she says, "there is a delay", she means
that delay is still happening - they are still

00:06:48.644 --> 00:06:50.914
losing time.

00:06:50.914 --> 00:06:56.774
But if she says "there has been a delay",
she means the delay started in the past and

00:06:56.774 --> 00:07:03.524
has continued up until the present. But as
we'll see, she is now fixing the problem.

00:07:03.524 --> 00:07:07.944
Well, they've managed to get another supplier
now. I suggested moving the completion date

00:07:07.944 --> 00:07:13.474
back. That way, there's no penalty, and they
agreed to re-deploy their workers until building

00:07:13.474 --> 00:07:17.664
can start again.
Let's look at the verb tenses here:

00:07:17.664 --> 00:07:23.534
"They've managed to get another supplier."
They managed to get another supplier in the

00:07:23.534 --> 00:07:28.024
past, and that supplier is still now supplying
the materials.

00:07:28.024 --> 00:07:34.373
I suggested moving the completion date back.
She suggested it at a particular time in the

00:07:34.373 --> 00:07:41.234
past.
There's no penalty. There is no penalty now.

00:07:41.234 --> 00:07:49.144
They agreed to redeploy their workers. They
agreed at a particular time in the past. Redeployed

00:07:49.144 --> 00:07:56.364
means they were sent to work somewhere else.
Look now at the last part of the scene.

00:07:56.364 --> 00:08:01.004
We've been waiting for the rain to stop, but
we can't control the weather!

00:08:01.004 --> 00:08:02.664
And the cost?

00:08:02.664 --> 00:08:07.134
At this stage, just a small overage. But I'll
be watching it very closely over the next

00:08:07.134 --> 00:08:13.063
few months. With no more delays, we're expecting
to complete the project just one month behind

00:08:13.063 --> 00:08:17.403
schedule.
Here we see some examples of continuous tenses

00:08:17.403 --> 00:08:22.873
to help meaning.
We've been waiting - We have been waiting

00:08:22.873 --> 00:08:30.433
for the rain to stop, and we are still waiting.
I'll be watching - I will be watching in the

00:08:30.433 --> 00:08:36.604
future over a long time.
We're expecting - we are expecting at the

00:08:36.604 --> 00:08:41.433
moment, and we will continue to expect in
the future.

00:08:41.433 --> 00:08:47.474
Notice also how Denise asks a question.
But we can't control the weather! And the

00:08:47.474 --> 00:08:52.623
cost?
She says, "and the cost?" The upward inflection

00:08:52.623 --> 00:08:58.964
in her voice, "and the cost?" tells us this
is a question, although it's not a complete

00:08:58.964 --> 00:09:06.274
sentence. The complete sentence would be,
"What will it cost?" In spoken English, this

00:09:06.274 --> 00:09:07.913
is very common.

00:09:07.913 --> 00:09:14.993
Practise some examples with Denise.
And the cost?

00:09:14.993 --> 00:09:18.663
And the result?

00:09:18.663 --> 00:09:25.964
And the reason?
Today we looked at reporting back. Remember,

00:09:25.964 --> 00:09:32.224
first summarise what you are going to report
on. We also focussed on the different verb

00:09:32.224 --> 00:09:34.753
tenses, which help exact meaning.

00:09:34.753 --> 00:09:41.154
That's all we have time for today, so I hope
we'll be seeing you next time for The Business

00:09:41.154 --> 00:09:41.894
of English.